<!--
  Pick-first navigate-to-pose tree, plus an on-demand "go to a fixed spot and
  place" branch triggered by a topic.

  MainTree = ReactiveFallback[ PlaceOnCommand, PickThenNavigate ]:
    * Every tick, PlaceOnCommand is checked FIRST (ReactiveFallback re-ticks
      from the top every time, unlike a plain Sequence/Fallback). While its
      TopicTrigger hasn't fired, it fails immediately (cheap) and the
      fallback falls through to PickThenNavigate as usual - the initial pick
      and normal navigation proceed untouched.
    * The moment /place_request goes false->true (while driving toward the
      original goal, carrying the object picked up at the very start),
      TopicTrigger fires once and PlaceOnCommand takes over. Because it now
      out-ranks PickThenNavigate in the ReactiveFallback, it PREEMPTS
      whatever PickThenNavigate was doing (its RUNNING nodes - e.g. the
      in-flight FollowPath - are halted/canceled) and runs a 3-leg detour:
        Leg 1: ComputePathToPose(spot A) -> OrientPathToYaw (pre-align the
               last 3m of the path to spot A's yaw) -> FollowPath, PlaceObject
        Leg 2: PushObstacle (arm extends to the push pose and HOLDS,
               gripper OPEN) -> OdomLimitedDrive (the BASE drives forward
               into the obstacle, using the held-out arm as a plow - this
               is what actually pushes it; stops once /odom shows
               `distance` ACTUALLY covered, not after a fixed time) ->
               RetractArm (folds the arm back to home) -> OdomLimitedDrive
               again (a short REVERSE clearance drive, so the retracted arm
               doesn't clip the obstacle during the rotate sweep next) ->
               nav2's Spin x3 (rotate sweep to help AMCL reconverge after
               the push) -> SetFixedGoal + ComputePathToPose + FollowPath
               to a fixed staging point well clear of any obstacle
               inflation (approaching spot A from close by kept stalling
               regardless of controller mechanism - see CAVEAT 1) ->
               ComputePathToPose({spot_a_goal}) + OrientPathToYaw +
               forward FollowPath (Leg 1's exact proven approach pattern,
               replayed from the staging point) -> FixedPickObject
               (re-pick object #1, still sitting where PlaceObject left it)
        Leg 3: ComputePathToPose({goal}) + FollowPath   (plan fresh from
                                             wherever the robot is now to
                                             the original goal - NOT a
                                             resume of {initial_path},
                                             which was found to end up
                                             empty here; see CAVEAT 3)
      If PlaceOnCommand SUCCEEDS (leg 3 finishes), the ReactiveFallback - and
      therefore the whole NavigateToPose action - succeeds right there; it
      never falls back to PickThenNavigate again for this goal.
      (Earlier versions of Leg 2: (1) drove to a separate spot B/spot C to
      pick-and-relocate the obstacle - used the arm 3x and extra navigation
      legs in a tight space, kept failing "Failed to make progress" when a
      leg landed on/near a point the robot had just placed an object on;
      (2) pushed by sweeping the ARM through the pick pose in place with the
      base stationary - mechanically ran fine but used the identical joint
      pose PlaceObject had just used, so it risked shoving the just-placed
      object instead of the separate obstacle. See git history for both.)
    * CAVEAT 1 (fixed, kept for context): DriveOnHeading's lookahead
      collision check (behavior_server's simulate_ahead_time, shared by
      EVERY DriveOnHeading/BackUp instance on the server, not per-plugin)
      reliably aborted the push with "Collision Ahead - Exiting
      DriveOnHeading" - correctly so, since driving into the obstacle on
      purpose is exactly what it exists to prevent. BackUp for the return
      leg hit the identical problem (backing toward the object just placed
      at spot A). Fixed in steps: (1) blind /cmd_vel driving with no
      collision check at all (first OpenLoopDrive, a fixed-time push/
      fixed-distance-mirrored reverse); (2) that still wasn't reliable -
      pushing against the obstacle's resistance meant the ACTUAL distance
      covered fell well short of the commanded one, so mirroring the same
      nominal distance on the way back overshot the real start position/
      yaw - fixed with RecordOdomPose/OdomLimitedDrive/DriveToRecordedPose,
      pure odometry dead-reckoning (record before the push, push until
      /odom shows `distance` truly covered, return via odom feedback);
      (3) pure odom dead-reckoning has ITS OWN failure mode: pushing
      against the obstacle's resistance makes the wheels slip, and if the
      slip isn't symmetric between the two sides, /odom integrates a false
      yaw drift that never physically happened, so the pose RecordOdomPose
      captured (and everything DriveToRecordedPose computed from it) could
      already be wrong. Tried recording via /amcl_pose instead (RecordMapPose
      - laser scan matching isn't fooled by wheel slip), but that has its
      OWN failure mode: AMCL only republishes /amcl_pose on qualifying
      motion (update_min_d/update_min_a), so while the robot sits still
      during PlaceObject's multi-second arm sequence, no fresh message ever
      arrives and the fresh subscription (started only once RecordMapPose
      ticks) times out - observed live as the whole Leg 2 failing before
      PushObstacle ever ran, falling back to CAVEAT 2's redundant pick.
      Fixed (4th revision) by realizing recording is unnecessary at all:
      spot A is already a FIXED, known map coordinate ({spot_a_goal}, set
      once by SetFixedGoal at the top of Leg 1) - the return leg just
      reuses it directly, instead of recording anything. (5th revision)
      Even with a fixed goal, trusting AMCL's pose estimate right after the
      push was unreliable: AMCL's motion model uses odom as its control
      input, and slip-corrupted odom during the push (plus straight-line
      motion being weak-observability for scan matching to correct) left a
      residual bias/divergence - the robot would confidently declare
      "arrived" while actually 1-2m short. A rotate sweep right after
      RetractArm (tried as a custom blind P-controller first - stalled on
      real skid-steer pivot-turn friction; nav2's own Spin worked instead)
      helps AMCL reconverge, and a subsequent odom-measured bulk backup
      (reliable for this short, pure straight-line, no-rotation-involved
      excursion) covers most of the remaining distance, so AMCL/map is only
      ever trusted for a short final stretch. (7th revision) Even that
      short final stretch, driven via a reverse-only DWB instance
      (FollowPathReverse + ComputeStraightPathToPose bypassing the global
      planner), kept stalling near the object - RotateToGoal doesn't
      handle an asymmetric reverse-only velocity range correctly ("No
      valid trajectories"), and even after removing it, BaseObstacle cost
      alone was apparently enough to make the robot crawl/dither and never
      close the last bit. Replaced with Leg 1's exact proven forward
      pattern instead (ComputePathToPose + OrientPathToYaw + FollowPath) -
      the object sits off to spot A's RIGHT, not on {spot_a_goal} itself,
      so the concern that originally motivated bypassing ComputePathToPose
      for this leg doesn't apply here. FollowPathReverse/
      ComputeStraightPathToPose/return_goal_checker are left registered in
      demo_nav2_params.yaml but unused, same convention as other
      superseded pieces (RecordOdomPose, RecordMapPose, etc.) - not
      deleted in case reverse-approach is worth revisiting.
      (8th revision) Even Leg 1's own pattern stalled the exact same way
      (goal accepted, then total silence - no "Passing new path to
      controller" at all - until the progress checker gave up) when
      started from only ~0.3-3m away (the odom bulk backup's end point).
      Same symptom independent of controller mechanism (reverse-only vs
      forward) pointed at the START position itself, not the controller -
      likely still within the pushed obstacle's/spot A's costmap
      inflation this close in. Fixed by not approaching from close by at
      all: SetFixedGoal + ComputePathToPose + FollowPath first drives out
      to a fixed staging point clear of any inflation, THEN the Leg-1-
      pattern approach to {spot_a_goal} runs from there - replaying the
      same "approach spot A from out in the open" trajectory shape that
      already works reliably the first time TopicTrigger fires, instead
      of inventing a new short final hop.
    * CAVEAT 2: if any leg FAILS, PlaceOnCommand fails, and the fallback
      drops back to PickThenNavigate - which, since it was halted mid-flight,
      restarts from FixedPickObject rather than resuming where it left off.
      Acceptable as a "give up on the detour, just keep doing the original
      mission" fallback, but be aware a failed detour costs you a redundant
      pick.
    * CAVEAT 3 (fixed, kept for context): Leg 3 used to resume {initial_path}
      (latched once by CaptureInitialPath, inside PickThenNavigate, before
      the detour ever starts). Twice in testing it came back empty (0 poses)
      by the time Leg 3 ran, tripping CAVEAT 2 above for a redundant pick
      every time. Leg 3 now plans fresh from {goal} instead - see its own
      comment below for why that's safe here.
    * EDIT before using: spot A's coordinate (Leg 1) is set below; Leg 2's
      dist_to_travel/speed/backup_dist/backup_speed are placeholders (0.3 m,
      0.1 m/s) - tune to how far the obstacle actually needs to move.
    * Fire it with (the -1 flag sends exactly one message, then exits):
        ros2 topic pub -1 /place_request std_msgs/msg/Bool "{data: true}"

  PickThenNavigate (memory Sequence):
    1. FixedPickObject - runs ONCE at the very start, before ComputePathToPose.
       Ported from fixed_pick_place.py::run_pick: drives the 4-DOF arm
       (FollowJointTrajectory) and gripper (GripperCommand) through a fixed
       joint-space sequence to pick up the object directly in front of the
       robot and hold it in a carry pose. Non-blocking (StatefulActionNode),
       so bt_navigator keeps ticking while the arm moves.
       If the pick FAILS, the whole navigate goal fails - wrap the node in
       <ForceSuccess> if you would rather drive on regardless.
    2. NavigateRecovery - the existing navigate-to-pose tree with replanning,
       recovery, and movable-obstacle handling (unchanged).

  Movable-obstacle handling (unchanged from custom_bt (copy).xml):
    * A VLM (outside this package) publishes two std_msgs/Bool topics:
        /obstacle/is_movable     - the blocker is a movable obstacle
        /obstacle/still_present  - that obstacle is still there
    * `rgbd_obstacle_localizer` publishes the obstacle pose in the map frame on
      /movable_obstacle/pose (only while is_movable is true). Its orientation is
      the estimated surface normal once perception fills it in; until then it is
      an identity quaternion.
    * CaptureInitialPath latches the FIRST global path for the goal.
    * ApproachObstacleNormal drives a short straight path to `standoff_distance`
      in front of the on-path obstacle. When /movable_obstacle/pose carries a
      real orientation it stops 0.2 m out along the surface normal and faces the
      obstacle; otherwise it falls back to the initial-path tangent (unchanged
      legacy behaviour).
    * WaitForObstacleClearance holds there until /obstacle/still_present is
      false, then the costmaps are wiped and the robot resumes {initial_path}.

  Perception is decoupled from the tick loop (design doc
  bt_perception_stabilizer_design.md):
    * The perception_stabilizer node (custom_nav2_bt_plugins) runs on its own,
      accumulating depth points, running PCA for the surface normal, and driving
      an IDLE/CANDIDATE/TRACKING state machine with time-window hysteresis. It
      publishes /perception_state (String), /obstacle_pose_stable (PoseStamped
      with the normal yaw) and /obstacle_pose_confidence (Float32).
    * IsObstacleTracking gates the branch on /perception_state == TRACKING, and
      transparently falls back to the legacy /obstacle/is_movable Bool when the
      stabilizer is not running.
    * LockApproachPose freezes ONE /obstacle_pose_stable sample the moment the
      branch commits (require_orientation=true -> waits for a converged normal)
      and re-emits it every tick; ApproachObstacleNormal consumes that frozen
      pose ({locked_obstacle_pose}), so the approach goal never drifts with
      perception noise and a dropout mid-approach cannot abort the branch.
-->
<root main_tree_to_execute="MainTree">
  <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
    <ReactiveFallback name="PlaceOnCommandOrPickThenNavigate">

      <Sequence name="PlaceOnCommand">
        <TopicTrigger topic="/place_request"/>

        <!-- ============================================================
             Leg 1: go straight to spot A, but pre-orient the final 3m of
             the PLANNED PATH itself to face spot A's yaw (0.0), instead of
             relying on DWB's terminal RotateToGoal-only phase to fix
             orientation after arriving. Two earlier approaches both failed:
             (1) tight yaw_goal_tolerance at spot A directly - RotateToGoal
             took 100+ seconds to close the last few degrees and kept
             getting killed by the (translation-only) progress checker
             before finishing, even after confirming it WAS genuinely
             converging (one run got to 0.2 deg residual, still timed out);
             (2) a standoff point + DriveOnHeading - same stall, just moved
             to wherever the standoff was, since the standoff goal itself
             still needed RotateToGoal to nail its own yaw.
             This replaces both: OrientPathToYaw drops the last
             align_distance meters of {spot_a_path_raw} and rebuilds that
             stretch as a fresh straight line of new waypoints (not just
             relabeling the orientation of NavFn's original points, whose
             positions can wobble slightly from grid discretization), all
             facing yaw=0.0, ending exactly at spot A.
             PathAlign/GoalAlign (scale 32/24, already high) then pull the
             robot to match that heading as part of NORMAL forward-driving
             scoring the whole final stretch, not a separate slow rotate-
             in-place mode - so by the time the robot is close enough to
             trigger RotateToGoal, the residual angle should already be
             small and finish quickly instead of needing to close a large
             angle from a standing start.
             장애물 좌표 (30.52300,-3.76110)에다가 base_link와 chassis의 offset(0.080[전방 오프셋])을 뺌
             ============================================================ -->
        <SetFixedGoal x="29.443" y="-3.59661" yaw="0.0" frame="map" goal="{spot_a_goal}"/>
        <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="ComputePathToSpotA">
          <ComputePathToPose goal="{spot_a_goal}" path="{spot_a_path_raw}" planner_id="GridBased"/>
          <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-SpotA"
                              service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
        </RecoveryNode>
        <OrientPathToYaw path="{spot_a_path_raw}" yaw="0.0" align_distance="3.0" oriented_path="{spot_a_path}"/>
        <FollowPath path="{spot_a_path}" controller_id="FollowPath" goal_checker_id="general_goal_checker"/>
        <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-SpotA"
                            service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
        <PlaceObject arm_action_name="/arm_controller/follow_joint_trajectory"
                     gripper_action_name="/gripper_controller/gripper_cmd"
                     arm_joint_names="joint1;joint2;joint3;joint4"
                     arm_move_time="3.0"
                     open_position="0.02501155674647538"
                     max_effort="0.0"
                     server_timeout="5.0"
                     step_timeout="15.0"
                     post_open_delay="1.0"/>

        <!-- ============================================================
             Leg 2 (simplified): push the obstacle aside IN PLACE - no
             navigation at all, the robot is already sitting at spot A.

             The original design here was a 3-leg detour (go to spot B and
             pick the obstacle up, go to spot C and place it, go BACK to
             spot A and re-pick object #1) using SetFixedGoal + Spin +
             ComputePathToPose/FollowPath, same as Leg 1/3 below. It used
             the arm 3x, spent a lot of time on planning/replanning in a
             tight space, and every navigation leg that landed on/near a
             point the robot itself had just placed an object on kept
             failing with "Failed to make progress" (DWB's RotateToGoal
             stalling against the very obstacle it needed to reach). See
             git history for that version if you want it back.

             Replaced with (4th revision): PushObstacle extends the arm to
             the push pose and HOLDS it there (gripper open, no grasp) -
             the actual push is the ROBOT BASE driving forward into the
             obstacle while the arm stays rigid, using the extended gripper
             as a plow. RetractArm then folds the arm back to home, and the
             robot returns to spot A by building a straight path (map
             frame) directly to {spot_a_goal} - the same fixed coordinate
             SetFixedGoal produced for Leg 1, reused as-is rather than
             recorded again - then FollowPath. See CAVEAT 1 above for the
             full history of why (went through odom dead-reckoning, then
             AMCL-snapshot dead-reckoning, before landing here). FixedPickObject
             then re-picks object #1, which should still be sitting where
             PlaceObject left it since the base is back at spot A.

             EDIT: `distance` on OdomLimitedDrive is a PLACEHOLDER
             (currently 3.0m, same convention as the 0.30/0.05 used in
             RecoveryActions further down) - tune to how far the obstacle
             actually needs to move to clear the original path.
             ComputeStraightPathToPose doesn't need its own distance - it
             just builds a path back to the recorded pose regardless of
             how far the push actually went.
             ============================================================ -->
        <!-- No snapshot needed before the push: spot A is already a fixed,
             known map coordinate ({spot_a_goal}, set by SetFixedGoal at the
             top of Leg 1) - reused as-is below for the return, instead of
             recording wherever the robot happens to be (which needed
             /amcl_pose or /odom and inherited their respective failure
             modes - AMCL not publishing while stationary, or odom drifting
             from wheel slip during the push). -->
        <PushObstacle arm_action_name="/arm_controller/follow_joint_trajectory"
                      gripper_action_name="/gripper_controller/gripper_cmd"
                      arm_joint_names="joint1;joint2;joint3;joint4"
                      arm_move_time="3.0"
                      open_position="0.02501155674647538"
                      max_effort="0.0"
                      push_hold_time="1.0"
                      server_timeout="5.0"
                      step_timeout="15.0"/>
        <!-- OdomLimitedDrive (not OpenLoopDrive): stops once /odom shows the
             robot has ACTUALLY covered `distance`, not after a fixed time -
             pushing against the obstacle's resistance meant the real
             distance covered was well short of what blind timing assumed. -->
        <OdomLimitedDrive cmd_vel_topic="/cmd_vel" odom_topic="/odom"
                          distance="3.0" speed="1.0" time_allowance="30.0"/>
        <RetractArm arm_action_name="/arm_controller/follow_joint_trajectory"
                    arm_joint_names="joint1;joint2;joint3;joint4"
                    arm_move_time="3.0"
                    server_timeout="5.0"
                    step_timeout="15.0"/>
        <!-- Extra clearance drive (separate from OdomLimitedDrive's push
             `distance` above, which is tuned to how far the OBSTACLE needs
             to move - not this) before rotating: right after the push, the
             robot is still close enough to the obstacle that even the
             retracted/home arm pose could clip it during WiggleInPlace's
             sweep below. MUST be negative (backward, toward spot A) - the
             obstacle was pushed FORWARD along with the base, so it's still
             right in front of the robot; driving forward again (the bug in
             the first version of this step) drove straight at it instead
             of away from it. speed is direction-agnostic in this node
             (cmd.linear.x = speed, distance is |traveled| via odom), so a
             negative speed here is exactly a short reverse. -->
        <OdomLimitedDrive cmd_vel_topic="/cmd_vel" odom_topic="/odom"
                          distance="0.5" speed="-0.3" time_allowance="10.0"/>
        <!-- Give AMCL fresh bearings to correct any residual bias before
             trusting its pose for the return: the push+drive-forward above
             is pure straight-line motion, which scan matching alone often
             can't fully correct if slip-corrupted odom skewed AMCL's
             motion-model prediction during the push. Net rotation ends
             back at 0, so it doesn't disturb the yaw=0.0 assumption the
             return leg depends on.

             Custom WiggleInPlace (blind /cmd_vel P-controller) tried first
             here and reliably stalled partway through every angle tested
             (0.3rad, then 0.2rad) - logging showed commanded angular.z
             staying large throughout while actual yaw asymptotically
             plateaued, the signature of a real physical stall (skid-steer
             pivot turns need much more torque than driving - wheels scrub
             the ground on all corners at once - and our modest blind
             command apparently couldn't push through it), not a tuning
             problem. Replaced with nav2's own Spin behavior (already
             loaded on behavior_server) - simpler, and its accel-limited
             control is more likely to actually get through the same
             friction than our hand-rolled P-controller. NOTE: Spin does
             its own local-costmap collision check (same mechanism that
             refused DriveOnHeading/BackUp earlier in this leg) - if IT
             also refuses here, that's useful signal the robot is still
             genuinely too close to something, not just under-powered. -->
        <Sequence name="WiggleForAmcl">
          <Spin spin_dist="0.2" time_allowance="10.0" is_recovery="false"/>
          <Spin spin_dist="-0.4" time_allowance="10.0" is_recovery="false"/>
          <Spin spin_dist="0.2" time_allowance="10.0" is_recovery="false"/>
        </Sequence>
        <!-- Return leg (8th revision): the short-hop versions of this
             (odom-measured bulk retreat then approach from ~0.3-3m out,
             tried both with a reverse-only controller and with Leg 1's
             forward pattern) BOTH hit the identical failure - the path
             computed fine, FollowPath accepted the goal, then total
             silence (no "Passing new path to controller" at all) until
             the progress checker gave up. Same symptom regardless of
             controller mechanism strongly suggests the problem isn't
             which controller - it's that the START of the approach was
             still within a costmap-complicated zone this close to
             spot A/the pushed obstacle's inflation.
             Fix: don't approach from close by at all - drive BACK OUT
             to a fixed staging point ({staging_goal}, chosen clear of any
             obstacle inflation) via a normal ComputePathToPose+FollowPath
             leg first, then re-run the SAME ComputePathToPose+
             OrientPathToYaw+FollowPath approach to {spot_a_goal} that
             already works reliably at the top of this whole detour (the
             very first time TopicTrigger fires, the robot is usually out
             in the open like this too) - i.e. replay that original
             approach trajectory shape instead of trying to invent a new
             short final hop. -->
        <SetFixedGoal x="24.5" y="-3.59661" yaw="0.0" frame="map" goal="{staging_goal}"/>
        <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="ComputePathToStaging">
          <ComputePathToPose goal="{staging_goal}" path="{staging_path}" planner_id="GridBased"/>
          <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-Staging"
                              service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
        </RecoveryNode>
        <FollowPath path="{staging_path}" controller_id="FollowPath" goal_checker_id="general_goal_checker"/>
        <!-- Reuse Leg 1's exact proven pattern (ComputePathToPose +
             OrientPathToYaw + forward FollowPath) for the actual approach
             to spot A, now starting from the clear staging point above
             instead of from close by. The concern that originally
             motivated bypassing ComputePathToPose here - a goal cell
             reading as lethal because the just-placed object sits on it -
             doesn't apply: the object actually sits off to spot A's
             RIGHT, not on top of {spot_a_goal} itself, so the global
             planner has a clear cell to path to. -->
        <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="ComputePathToSpotA-Return">
          <ComputePathToPose goal="{spot_a_goal}" path="{return_path_raw}" planner_id="GridBased"/>
          <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-SpotAReturn"
                              service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
        </RecoveryNode>
        <OrientPathToYaw path="{return_path_raw}" yaw="0.0" align_distance="3.0" oriented_path="{return_path}"/>
        <FollowPath path="{return_path}" controller_id="FollowPath" goal_checker_id="general_goal_checker"/>
        <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-SpotAReturn"
                            service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
        <FixedPickObject arm_action_name="/arm_controller/follow_joint_trajectory"
                         gripper_action_name="/gripper_controller/gripper_cmd"
                         arm_joint_names="joint1;joint2;joint3;joint4"
                         arm_move_time="3.0"
                         open_position="0.02501155674647538"
                         grasp_position="0.002"
                         max_effort="0.0002"
                         server_timeout="5.0"
                         step_timeout="15.0"/>

        <!-- ============================================================
             Leg 3: plan a FRESH path from wherever the robot is right now
             to the original goal ({goal} - set by nav2 when the
             NavigateToPose action was accepted, untouched by any of our
             custom legs, so it is always valid) and follow it.

             Originally this resumed {initial_path} (the pre-detour path
             latched by CaptureInitialPath at the very start of navigation),
             but that node lives inside PickThenNavigate/NavigateRecovery,
             which gets halted the instant PlaceOnCommand takes over and
             was never ticked again - twice now this produced an EMPTY
             {initial_path} (0 poses) here, aborting FollowPath immediately
             and triggering CAVEAT 2 (a redundant extra pick). Root cause
             not fully nailed down, but planning fresh from {goal} sidesteps
             it entirely and is simpler besides.
             ============================================================ -->
        <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-ResumeInitial"
                            service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
        <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-ResumeInitial"
                            service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
        <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="ComputePathToGoal-Resume">
          <ComputePathToPose goal="{goal}" path="{resume_path}" planner_id="GridBased"/>
          <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-Resume-Retry"
                              service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
        </RecoveryNode>
        <FollowPath path="{resume_path}" controller_id="FollowPath" goal_checker_id="general_goal_checker"/>
      </Sequence>

      <Sequence name="PickThenNavigate">

      <FixedPickObject arm_action_name="/arm_controller/follow_joint_trajectory"
                       gripper_action_name="/gripper_controller/gripper_cmd"
                       arm_joint_names="joint1;joint2;joint3;joint4"
                       arm_move_time="3.0"
                       open_position="0.02501155674647538"
                       grasp_position="-0.002"
                       max_effort="0.0002"
                       server_timeout="5.0"
                       step_timeout="15.0"/>

      <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="6" name="NavigateRecovery">
        <PipelineSequence name="NavigateWithReplanning">
          <RateController hz="1.0">
            <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="ComputePathToPose">
              <ComputePathToPose goal="{goal}" path="{path}" planner_id="GridBased"/>
              <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-Context" service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
            </RecoveryNode>
          </RateController>
          <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="FollowPath">
            <ReactiveFallback name="FollowPathOrHandleMovableObstacle">
              <Sequence name="HandleMovableObstacle">
                <CaptureInitialPath path="{path}" goal="{goal}"
                                    goal_change_threshold="0.5"
                                    initial_path="{initial_path}"/>
                <IsObstacleTracking state_topic="/perception_state"
                                    tracking_value="TRACKING"
                                    state_timeout="1.0"
                                    fallback_topic="/obstacle/is_movable"
                                    fallback_timeout="3.0"/>
                <LockApproachPose pose_topic="/obstacle_pose_stable"
                                  pose_timeout="2.0"
                                  acquire_timeout="8.0"
                                  require_orientation="true"
                                  locked_pose="{locked_obstacle_pose}"/>
                <ApproachObstacleNormal path="{initial_path}"
                                        obstacle_pose="{locked_obstacle_pose}"
                                        pose_topic="/movable_obstacle/pose"
                                        standoff_distance="0.2"
                                        use_surface_normal="true"
                                        face_obstacle="true"
                                        on_path_tolerance="0.35"
                                        pose_timeout="2.0"
                                        recompute_threshold="0.2"
                                        global_frame="map"
                                        robot_base_frame="base_link"
                                        approach_path="{approach_path}"
                                        approach_goal="{approach_goal}"/>
                <Sequence name="ApproachThenResume">
                  <FollowPath path="{approach_path}" controller_id="FollowPath" goal_checker_id="general_goal_checker"/>
                  <WaitForObstacleClearance topic="/obstacle/still_present"
                                            timeout="0.0"
                                            clear_on_stale="true"
                                            message_timeout="3.0"/>
                  <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Resume"
                                      service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
                  <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-Resume"
                                      service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
                  <FollowPath path="{initial_path}" controller_id="FollowPath" goal_checker_id="general_goal_checker"/>
                </Sequence>
              </Sequence>
              <FollowPath path="{path}" controller_id="FollowPath" goal_checker_id="general_goal_checker"/>
            </ReactiveFallback>
            <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Context" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
          </RecoveryNode>
        </PipelineSequence>
        <ReactiveFallback name="RecoveryFallback">
          <GoalUpdated/>
          <RoundRobin name="RecoveryActions">
            <Sequence name="ClearingActions">
              <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Subtree" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
              <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-Subtree" service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
            </Sequence>
            <Spin spin_dist="1.57"/>
            <Wait wait_duration="5"/>
            <BackUp backup_dist="0.30" backup_speed="0.05"/>
          </RoundRobin>
        </ReactiveFallback>
      </RecoveryNode>

      </Sequence>

    </ReactiveFallback>
  </BehaviorTree>
</root>
